<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:antiSelfWarrant> "The implementation unit and the conformance unit are separate semantic work units, dispatched independently. An implementation therefore does not author the evidence that satisfies it, and the facet evaluator reads the executed outcome rather than any assertion the producing unit made." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:authoredVersusGenerated> "ANQR capability modules and their conformance tests are model-authored under graph-derived paths and graph-derived semantics. They are generated under authority rather than hand-authored, and no model contributes a path, filename or module boundary." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:canonicalName> "v1" <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:cohesionRule> "Every outstanding applicable implementation-conformance facet of one ANQR capability describes the behaviour of that capability's single module, so those facets form exactly one implementation work unit. A facet boundary is a requirement boundary, not a work boundary." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:conformancePathRule> <urn:anqr:delivery-path-rule:capabilityconformance> <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:dependencyDerivationRule> "Exactly one implementation dependency edge exists per capability: the conformance unit requires the concrete module, because a behavioural test cannot be written against a surface that does not yet exist. No ANQR capability implementation depends on another, so the ready frontier is every capability with outstanding work." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:filenameConvention> "One module per capability named exactly the capability canonicalName with a .ts extension, under a directory named for the capability's domain. The conformance test carries the same stem with a .conformance.test.ts extension. No other filename is authorised and no additional file may be created by a work unit." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:implementationPathRule> <urn:anqr:delivery-path-rule:capabilityimplementation> <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "The paths it derives are relative to the ANQR product repository. Realisation under this convention can never write into USF Core, because the write root is the authenticated product root and the product declares the prefixes it permits." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:nonClaim> "This convention states where ANQR product code belongs and how work is decomposed. It asserts nothing about whether any ANQR capability is implemented, and it is evidence for no facet." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:outputRule> "An ANQR capability module is a self-contained TypeScript module with no runtime dependency outside the Node standard library and its own capability directory. It exports named functions and the declared state and error identifiers its facets require, so the behaviour every facet statement asserts is directly invocable from a test." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:ownedByRepository> <urn:anqr:repository:main> <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:pathDerivation> "canonicalPath is the governing pathPattern taken relative to the ANQR product repository root, with {domain} the local name of the capability inDomain object and {capability} the capability canonicalName. Both substitutions come from the graph, so a model never proposes, chooses or influences a path. Where that repository sits on a given machine is operator state resolved through its locator, never a path in the graph." <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
<urn:anqr:capability-implementation-convention:v1:1> <urn:usf:predicate:type> <urn:usf:type:CapabilityImplementationConvention> <urn:anqr:graph:realisation-convention> .
